# =====================================================================
#  bench.params  --  PX4 params for the Pixhawk 6C Mini PROPS-OFF BENCH
#  (interceptor "brain bench", build_tab subsystem `brain`, steps brn-01..brn-06)
# =====================================================================
#
#  PURPOSE
#  -------
#  Bring up the Pi 5 <-> Pixhawk 6C Mini MAVLink/OFFBOARD path on the desk,
#  BEFORE any airframe exists, so the deploy loop's one sim-unvalidated link
#  (flight/deploy/seeker_loop.py MAVSDK over a REAL serial port) is retired
#  while the tripod campaign runs in parallel. See:
#    - docs/project_state.json build_tab.subsystems[brain] (steps brn-01..06)
#    - docs/hardware_order_list.md  §3 (compatibility checklist) + §B (RC path)
#    - flight/deploy/seeker_loop.py + scripts/check_deploy_bench.sh
#
#  VERIFIED AGAINST PX4 v1.16.0  (pre-install review, 2026-07-26)
#  --------------------------------------------------------------
#  The board on the bench runs PX4 v1.16.0 (QGC "Firmware Version 1.16.0").
#  Every line below was re-checked against the v1.16 parameter reference
#  (https://docs.px4.io/v1.16/en/advanced_config/parameter_reference.html)
#  and, where behaviour mattered, against the v1.16.0 source. What changed:
#    - COM_ARM_WO_GPS no longer means "allow arming without GPS". In v1.16 it
#      is the ACTION taken when the EKF2_GPS_CHECK preflight check fails
#      (0 = Deny arming, 1 = Warning only, 2 = Disabled). Value 0 is still the
#      right one for "GPS required to arm", but the old wording was wrong.
#    - COM_KILL_DISARM's v1.16 DEFAULT is 5.0 s (the file used to claim 0).
#      The line is kept as an explicit assertion; on a stock v1.16 board it is
#      a no-op.
#    - MAV_1_MODE = 2 and SER_TEL2_BAUD = 921600 are ALSO the v1.16 defaults
#      (instance 1 defaults to Onboard; TELEM2 defaults to 921600). Kept
#      explicit so the intent is readable, but they change nothing.
#    - RC_MAP_KILL_SW is now 0 (Unassigned) instead of a GUESSED channel 5 --
#      see the WHY in section (2). This is the one value that changed meaning.
#    - MAV_1_FLOW_CTRL = 0 ADDED: the TELEM2 link is wired with 3 leads, so
#      CTS/RTS do not exist and flow control must not be left on auto.
#    - CBRK_IO_SAFETY = 0 ADDED (2026-07-26, second pass). This file used to say
#      the safety switch was "kept active" because we deliberately did NOT set
#      the breaker. That was INVERTED: 22027 (= safety BYPASSED) IS the compiled
#      default -- v1.16.0 src/lib/circuit_breaker/circuit_breaker_params.c:70
#      PARAM_DEFINE_INT32(CBRK_IO_SAFETY, 22027) -- so a stock board ships with
#      the safety-button arming gate ALREADY OFF. See section (2).
#    - RC_INPUT_PROTO: the old comment claimed "stays at its default (0 = auto)".
#      Wrong twice: 0 means None (RC DISABLED), and the parameter does not exist
#      on this firmware at all. See section (2).
#
#  SCOPE / SAFETY POSTURE
#  ----------------------
#  Bench = FC on the desk, NO ESC, NO motors, NO airframe -- NOTHING can spin.
#  This profile deliberately keeps arming HARD (safety button REQUIRED, via the
#  CBRK_IO_SAFETY=0 line in section (2) -- it is NOT the firmware default -- plus
#  GPS required + strict default checks). The bench OFFBOARD check itself
#  (check_deploy_bench.sh) does NOT arm -- it verifies the MAVLink connect,
#  own-state stream, OFFBOARD mode switch, and setpoint stream with the vehicle
#  DISARMED. Two v1.16 facts that make that safe, both source-verified:
#    - a mode change is ALWAYS allowed while disarmed, so OFFBOARD is accepted
#      even with no GPS fix and no RC  (v1.16.0 src/modules/commander/
#      UserModeIntention.cpp: "Always allow mode change while disarmed");
#    - NO failsafe action is ever executed while disarmed  (v1.16.0
#      src/modules/commander/failsafe/framework.cpp: `if (!state.armed) {
#      returned_state.action = Action::None; return; }`), so the RED Radio /
#      absent receiver cannot trigger an RC-loss failsafe that fights the test.
#
#  HOW TO LOAD (see configs/px4_6cmini/README.md for the full procedure)
#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#    0. FIRST set the airframe in QGC (Vehicle Setup > Airframe), then reboot.
#       ORDER MATTERS, and here is the real mechanism: QGC's Airframe "Apply"
#       writes SYS_AUTOSTART *and* SYS_AUTOCONFIG=1 (QGC
#       AirframeComponentController::changeAutostart), and on the next boot
#       PX4's rcS does `param reset_all SYS_AUTOSTART SYS_PARAM_VER RC* CAL_*
#       COM_FLTMODE* LND_FLIGHT* TC_* COM_FLIGHT*` (v1.16.0 ROMFS/px4fmu_common/
#       init.d/rcS). Everything in THIS file except RC_MAP_* would be wiped.
#       => Airframe first, then this file. And if you ever CHANGE the airframe
#          later, RE-LOAD this file afterwards.
#       (Any generic multicopter airframe lights up the MC + OFFBOARD stack for
#        the plumbing test: 4001 "Generic Quadcopter" (Quadrotor x) or 5001
#        "Generic Quad + geometry" (Quadrotor +) are equally fine with no motors
#        attached. The REAL airframe ID, geometry, motors and tuning are set when
#        the physical frame is built.)
#    1. QGC > Vehicle Setup > Parameters > Tools > "Load from file" > this file.
#    2. Reboot the FC (SDLOG_MODE and every MAV_*/SER_* line below are
#       reboot-required). Re-open Parameters and confirm each value.
#
#  FILE FORMAT (QGroundControl parameter file)
#  -------------------------------------------
#  TAB-separated columns:  <sysid> <compid> <PARAM_NAME> <VALUE> <MAV_PARAM_TYPE>
#    MAV_PARAM_TYPE: 6 = INT32,  9 = REAL32 (float).
#  Lines beginning with '#' are comments (ignored by the loader). Verified
#  against QGC's own parser (ParameterEditorController::buildDiffFromFile):
#  it skips '#' and blank lines and accepts exactly this 5-field layout.
#  Params whose file value already equals the vehicle value are simply skipped,
#  so the no-op lines flagged above will not appear in QGC's diff dialog.
#  Every data line has a WHY + a doc source. Lines marked "(= v1.16 default)"
#  are explicit assertions, not changes.
#  Params that must WAIT for the airframe are listed (commented) at the bottom.
#  CAUTION on that convention: a parameter existing in the online v1.16 reference
#  does NOT mean it exists on px4_fmu-v6c -- that reference is the UNION over all
#  boards, and PX4 params are module-scoped, so a param whose module this board
#  does not build is simply unknown here (RC_INPUT_PROTO, section (2)). Check the
#  board's default.px4board before writing a "(= v1.16 default)" assertion line.
#
# Onboard parameters for Vehicle 1
#
# Stack: PX4
# Vehicle: Multirotor
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  (1) TELEM2 companion-computer MAVLink link  (Pi 5 GPIO14/15 <-> TELEM2)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Put a 2nd MAVLink instance (index 1) on TELEM2 in the "Onboard" profile at
#  921600 baud -- the exact link the deploy loop rides. TELEM2 is UART5
#  (/dev/ttyS3) on the 6C Mini, confirmed by BOTH the board doc
#  (docs.px4.io/v1.16/en/flight_controller/pixhawk6c_mini: UART5 | /dev/ttyS3 |
#  TELEM2) and the firmware's own board file (v1.16.0 boards/px4/fmu-v6c/
#  default.px4board: CONFIG_BOARD_SERIAL_TEL2="/dev/ttyS3").
#  Wire ONLY 3 leads (TX/RX crossed + GND); do NOT wire TELEM2 pin-1 (+5V) to
#  the Pi -- the Pi is on its own rail (two-rail rule) and +5V would destroy the
#  3.3V GPIO.
#  Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 Serial Port Config + MAVLink Peripherals + RPi
#          Companion; project baud fixed at 921600 by hardware_order_list.md
#          §3/§B + build_tab.
#
#  MAV_1_CONFIG = 102 -> assign MAVLink instance 1 to the TELEM2 serial port.
#     102 = "TELEM 2" is confirmed twice: the v1.16 param reference lists
#     102 : TELEM 2 for MAV_1_CONFIG, and the generator that MAKES that enum
#     (v1.16.0 Tools/serial/generate_config.py) has "TEL2": index 102.
1	1	MAV_1_CONFIG	102	6
#  MAV_1_MODE = 2 -> "Onboard": the companion-computer message set the seeker
#     loop reads as own-state. Verified this mode actually streams what MAVSDK
#     needs (v1.16.0 src/modules/mavlink/mavlink_main.cpp, case
#     MAVLINK_MODE_ONBOARD): ATTITUDE 100 Hz, ATTITUDE_QUATERNION 50 Hz,
#     LOCAL_POSITION_NED 30 Hz, ALTITUDE 10 Hz, GLOBAL_POSITION_INT 50 Hz,
#     plus HEARTBEAT/SYS_STATUS -- and setpoints are accepted inbound on any
#     mode. (= v1.16 default for instance 1; kept explicit.)
1	1	MAV_1_MODE	2	6
#  MAV_1_FLOW_CTRL = 0 -> FORCE hardware flow control OFF on this instance.
#     WHY THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE: the link is 3-wire, so TELEM2's CTS/RTS
#     pins are left floating. The default (2 = Auto-detected) does NOT mean
#     "start without flow control" -- v1.16.0 mavlink_main.cpp opens the port
#     with CRTSCTS SET and only drops it after the TX buffer has backed up with
#     no successful write for >500 ms. With a floating CTS that is at best a
#     ~0.5 s stall on every boot and at worst a silent dead link. Forcing 0
#     matches the physical wiring exactly. Values: 0 Force off / 1 Force on /
#     2 Auto-detected. Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 parameter reference.
1	1	MAV_1_FLOW_CTRL	0	6
#  MAV_1_FORWARD = 1 -> forward messages between this companion link and the GCS
#     link (USB / TELEM1) so QGC can watch the Pi's traffic during bring-up.
#     NOTE: the PX4 companion-computer guide recommends FORWARD *disabled* for
#     a companion link, and 0 is the v1.16 default -- this is a deliberate BENCH
#     convenience, not a flight setting. It is safe: PX4 never forwards a
#     message back onto the link it came from and only forwards to a link whose
#     peer has been seen (mavlink_main.cpp Mavlink::forward_message). Cosmetic
#     side effect: MAV_HB_FORW_EN defaults to Enabled, so QGC may show the Pi's
#     MAVSDK component as an extra MAVLink node. Turn this to 0 for flight.
1	1	MAV_1_FORWARD	1	6
#  SER_TEL2_BAUD = 921600 -> TELEM2 line rate; MUST match the Pi side + the
#     --mavsdk-url baud (serial:///dev/ttyAMA0:921600) and scripts/
#     check_deploy_bench.sh's BENCH_BAUD default (921600). 921600 is a listed
#     enum value ("921600 8N1"). (= v1.16 default for TELEM2; kept explicit.)
1	1	SER_TEL2_BAUD	921600	6
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  (2) SBUS RC in + kill switch + arming kept HARD  (brn-05 "kill switch honored")
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  RC path (hardware_order_list.md §B, constraint no-datalink): the ELRS RX is
#  flashed to SBUS OUTPUT and wired to the 6C Mini's DEDICATED RC IN pad (NOT a
#  UART -- stock PX4 has no CRSF driver, and the Mini's UARTs are spoken for by
#  GPS1/TELEM1-SiK/TELEM2-Pi).
#
#  DO NOT ADD AN RC_INPUT_PROTO LINE -- and note that this is the ONE param in
#  this file that must never get a "(= v1.16 default)" assertion.
#  [CORRECTED 2026-07-26: this comment used to say "RC_INPUT_PROTO stays at its
#  default (0 = auto)". Wrong twice over.]
#    (a) The param does not exist on this firmware. RC on the 6C Mini is decoded
#        by the PX4IO CO-PROCESSOR, which gathers SBUS + PPM + DSM/ST24/SUMD in
#        PARALLEL every cycle -- genuinely auto-detected, with no protocol
#        parameter (v1.16.0 src/modules/px4iofirmware/controls.cpp: sbus_input,
#        ppm_input, dsm_port_input, results OR'd). PX4IO is present on the MINI:
#        boards/px4/fmu-v6c/src/manifest.c maps V6C21/V6C22 ("MINI") to
#        hw_mft_list_v0600 with PX4_MFT_PX4IO present=1 mandatory=1, and
#        default.px4board sets CONFIG_DRIVERS_PX4IO=y. RC_INPUT_PROTO belongs to
#        the FMU-side rc_input driver, which fmu-v6c does NOT build (no
#        CONFIG_DRIVERS_RC_INPUT in default.px4board; src/drivers/rc_input/Kconfig
#        is "default n"), so a PARAM_SET for it is rejected as an unknown param.
#    (b) On boards that DO build rc_input, the v1.16 default is -1 (Auto) and
#        0 means None -- i.e. RC INPUT DISABLED, silently and permanently, with
#        no error printed anywhere. Writing 0 here "to make the default explicit"
#        would have been the exact opposite of the intent.
#  After binding, run QGC Radio calibration; that is where the kill-switch
#  channel gets its real number. ELRS is kill/ARM ONLY -- it carries NO guidance.
#
#  RC_MAP_KILL_SW = 0 -> Emergency kill switch DELIBERATELY UNASSIGNED for now.
#     CHANGED 2026-07-26 (was a placeholder 5). Three reasons, in order:
#     (a) A GUESSED mapping is not protection, it is the ILLUSION of protection.
#         Until QGC Radio calibration says which channel the RadioMaster Pocket
#         switch lands on, any number here is unverified.
#     (b) The guess was actively risky: on ELRS/SBUS, channel 5 = AUX1 is the
#         CONVENTIONAL ARM channel, and PX4 reads kill as engaged when that
#         channel sits above RC_KILLSWITCH_TH (v1.16 default 0.75, positive
#         polarity = "true when channel > threshold"). Mapping kill onto the arm
#         channel means the first arm gesture after binding would look like
#         "Kill switch engaged" -- the exact confusing failure this bench does
#         not need.
#     (c) It costs nothing on the bench either way: with NO receiver attached
#         PX4 publishes manual_control_switches ONLY while RC is present and
#         regularly updating (v1.16.0 src/modules/rc_update/rc_update.cpp:
#         "only publish manual control if the signal is present and regularly
#         updating"), so channel 5 could not read as anything -- neutral,
#         failsafe or otherwise. No spurious kill was possible; there was simply
#         no upside to carrying a wrong number.
#     ACTION REQUIRED LATER: after binding + Radio calibration, set this to the
#     REAL channel and confirm QGC reads "Kill switch" active when you flip it.
#     Until then this vehicle has NO mapped kill switch -- which is honest, and
#     harmless while nothing can spin. Values: 0 = Unassigned, 1..18 = Channel n.
#     Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 parameter reference (RC_MAP_KILL_SW,
#     RC_KILLSWITCH_TH) + Arm/Disarm/Prearm.
1	1	RC_MAP_KILL_SW	0	6
#  COM_KILL_DISARM = 5.0 s -> auto-DISARM 5 s after a kill, so a killed vehicle
#     cannot be silently re-activated by releasing the switch. CORRECTED
#     2026-07-26: this file used to say the default was 0 ("never auto-disarm").
#     It is not -- the v1.16 default IS 5.0 s (min 0, max 30, unit s), so this
#     line asserts the default rather than changing it. Kept for explicitness.
#     NOT OBSERVABLE ON THIS BENCH (2026-07-26): this 5 s timer is ARMED-ONLY --
#     v1.16.0 Commander::handleAutoDisarm() runs the whole kill/auto-disarm block
#     inside `if (isArmed())`, so on a never-armed bench it is structurally
#     unreachable. What you CAN see disarmed is "Kill engaged"/"Kill disengaged"
#     in QGC and actuator_armed.manual_lockdown toggling in the ULog. The
#     auto-disarm half is DEFERRED to the first props-off arm test on the frame.
#     Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 parameter reference (COM_KILL_DISARM).
1	1	COM_KILL_DISARM	5.000000	9
#  COM_ARM_WO_GPS = 0 -> "Deny arming" when the GPS preflight check fails.
#     CORRECTED 2026-07-26: in v1.16 this param is NOT "allow arming without
#     GPS" -- it is "GPS preflight check: measures taken when a check defined by
#     EKF2_GPS_CHECK is failing", with 0 = Deny arming, 1 = Warning only
#     (default), 2 = Disabled. Setting 0 still gets what we want: no arming on a
#     bad/absent position solution, which blocks casual indoor arming.
#     NOTE WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* DO: it does not gate this bench. Nothing arms
#     here, and the disarmed OFFBOARD switch does not need a GPS fix at all (see
#     the SAFETY POSTURE block above). Do NOT go outside chasing a fix for the
#     brn-05 run. Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 parameter reference (COM_ARM_WO_GPS).
1	1	COM_ARM_WO_GPS	0	6
#
#  CBRK_IO_SAFETY = 0 -> safety button REQUIRED to arm.
#     ⛔ CORRECTED 2026-07-26 -- THIS BLOCK PREVIOUSLY SAID THE OPPOSITE. It read
#     "no param line -- a deliberate NON-action: we KEEP the safety switch active
#     (do NOT set CBRK_IO_SAFETY=22027)". That inverts the polarity: 22027 IS the
#     COMPILED DEFAULT (v1.16.0 src/lib/circuit_breaker/circuit_breaker_params.c:
#     PARAM_DEFINE_INT32(CBRK_IO_SAFETY, 22027)), and 22027 means the breaker is
#     TRIPPED, i.e. safety BYPASSED. Safety::Safety() then sets _button_available
#     = true and _safety_off = true at construction and the "Press safety button
#     first" arming check can never fire (v1.16.0 src/modules/commander/
#     Safety.cpp). So a stock-flashed 6C Mini ships with the safety-button arming
#     gate ALREADY OFF, and the pack's written guarantee was false. Setting 0
#     puts the gate back. Values: 0 = safety enforced, 22027 = bypassed.
#     ACCEPT THIS TRADEOFF KNOWINGLY -- it is not free:
#       * Nothing about TODAY'S bench changes: the props-off OFFBOARD check never
#         arms, and a mode change is always allowed while disarmed.
#       * But it does NOT mean "no effect until an M10 is attached". px4io holds a
#         ButtonPublisher whose CONSTRUCTOR advertises the safety_button topic
#         unconditionally (v1.16.0 src/lib/button/ButtonPublisher.cpp), so as soon
#         as px4io starts, Safety::safetyButtonHandler() sets _button_available =
#         true even with no button wired -- and systemCheck.cpp's gate
#         (`safety_button_available && !safety_off && !isArmed()`) then raises
#         "Preflight Fail: Press safety button first" permanently. Expect QGC to
#         show a standing arming-check failure, and expect the board to be
#         UNARMABLE until an M10 with a safety switch is on GPS1 and pressed.
#         That is the correct fail-closed direction for a props-off bench, and it
#         means a deliberate props-off ARM test needs the M10 present (or this
#         line temporarily set back to 22027, knowingly).
#       * REBOOT REQUIRED IN PRACTICE, and QGC will NOT prompt for it: the param
#         carries no @reboot_required tag, but Safety.cpp reads it ONLY in the
#         constructor, at commander start. The load procedure already reboots.
#       * Being CBRK_*, it is NOT in rcS's `param reset_all` exclude list, so an
#         airframe change WIPES it like the rest of this file -- re-load then.
#     VERIFY IN 10 SECONDS ON THE LIVE BOARD: QGC > Parameters > CBRK_IO_SAFETY.
#     Source: v1.16.0 source as cited; docs.px4.io/v1.16 Arm/Disarm/Prearm.
1	1	CBRK_IO_SAFETY	0	6
#  COM_RC_IN_MODE also stays at its default -- but note the v1.16 default is 3
#  ("RC or Joystick keep first"), NOT 0 ("RC Transmitter only") as this file
#  used to claim. Either way RC input is accepted and a mapped kill switch is
#  live once a receiver exists. Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 (COM_RC_IN_MODE).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  (3) Log the disarmed bench session (ULogs are the metrology, brn-01)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SDLOG_MODE = 2 -> log "from boot until shutdown". The bench runs DISARMED, and
#     the default (0 = when armed until disarm) would capture NOTHING -- so we
#     log from boot to get the OFFBOARD-disarmed session on the microSD as bench
#     evidence. Values: -1 disabled / 0 when armed until disarm (default) /
#     1 from boot until disarm / 2 from boot until shutdown / 3 while AUX1 >30%
#     / 4 from 1st armed until shutdown. REBOOT REQUIRED for this one.
#     Confirm a microSD is inserted (brn-01).
#     Source: docs.px4.io/v1.16 parameter reference (SDLOG_MODE).
1	1	SDLOG_MODE	2	6
#
# =============================================================================
#  NOT NEEDED FOR THIS BENCH -- checked and deliberately left alone
# =============================================================================
#  Written down because "is something missing for a no-RC props-off OFFBOARD
#  bench?" is the question this file most deserves to be asked, and the answer
#  is source-verified NO:
#    - COM_RCL_EXCEPT (RC-loss exceptions, bit 2 = Offboard) and NAV_RCL_ACT
#      (RC-loss action, default 2 = Return): NOT needed here. PX4 v1.16 runs no
#      failsafe action at all while DISARMED (failsafe/framework.cpp), and the
#      RC-loss action is additionally suppressed when manual control was already
#      lost at arm time (failsafe.cpp updateArmingState: "ensure action isn't
#      added while disarmed"). A RED radio with no receiver therefore cannot
#      fight this test. These become a real FLIGHT decision once the interceptor
#      flies OFFBOARD with ELRS present -- decide them then, with the airframe.
#    - COM_OF_LOSS_T (1.0 s) / COM_OBL_RC_ACT (0 = Position mode): the offboard-
#      loss fallback. It will "fire" when the setpoint stream ends at the end of
#      the run, and while disarmed that is a no-op mode change. Expect a benign
#      QGC message at the end of the run; it is not a failure.
#    - A GPS fix / the M10: not required (see COM_ARM_WO_GPS above). The vehicle
#      code path (seeker_loop.run_mavsdk with smoke=False) hard-requires only
#      the EKF ATTITUDE stream, which needs no GPS; missing altitude is a loud
#      warning, not a failure. The health/global-position wait is inside the
#      SITL-only `smoke` branch.
#
# =============================================================================
#  WAITS FOR THE AIRFRAME -- do NOT set on the bench (no ESC/motors/battery yet)
# =============================================================================
#  These are set when the physical Tier-2 frame is built + tuned; setting them
#  now is meaningless-to-dangerous on a bare FC. Listed so the boundary is explicit:
#    - SYS_AUTOSTART           final airframe ID + its motor geometry (bench uses
#                              a generic quad set from the Airframe tab -- and
#                              NEVER from this file, see the header for why).
#    - CA_* / actuator outputs motor mixing + geometry (control allocation).
#    - DSHOT_CONFIG / PWM_*    ESC protocol + PWM ranges (no ESC on the bench).
#    - BAT1_N_CELLS, BAT1_*    battery cell count + capacity + V/I calibration
#                              (bench runs on USB, not the PM02 pack; QGC
#                              currently reads "Number of Cells 0" -- expected).
#    - MC_* (rate/att PIDs)    flight tuning -- only meaningful on the real frame.
#    - MPC_* limits, MIS/NAV   flight limits + missions -- airframe/flight phase.
#    - RC_MAP_KILL_SW (real)   the verified channel, after Radio calibration.
#    - COM_RCL_EXCEPT / NAV_RCL_ACT  RC-loss behaviour while flying OFFBOARD.
#  SENSORS-ONLY tasks that ARE done on the bench are QGC procedures, not params:
#  accel/gyro/mag/level calibration + Radio calibration (after ELRS bind).
# =============================================================================
